      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of the numbers 18 and 42, and then compute their product.\n\nFirst, we find the prime factorizations of 18 and 42:\n\\[\n18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\\[\n42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n\\]\n\nThe GCD is the product of the lowest power of each prime factor present in both factorizations:\n\\[\n\\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n\\]\n\nThe LCM is the product of the highest power of each prime factor appearing in the factorizations:\n\\[\n\\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the product of the GCD and the LCM:\n\\[\n\\text{Product} = \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126 = 756\n\\]\n\nThus, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of 18 and 42 is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
