      "text": "To find the product of the greatest common divisor (GCD) and the least common multiple (LCM) of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\), we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the prime factorizations of the numbers:**\n\n   \\[\n   18 = 2 \\times 3^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   42 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the GCD:**\n\n   The GCD is found by taking the lowest power of all prime factors present in both numbers.\n\n   - The prime factor \\(2\\) appears in both factorizations but with the lowest power \\(2^1\\).\n   - The prime factor \\(3\\) appears in both factorizations but with the lowest power \\(3^1\\).\n\n   Therefore, the GCD of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{GCD} = 2^1 \\times 3^1 = 2 \\times 3 = 6\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the LCM:**\n\n   The LCM is found by taking the highest power of all prime factors present in either number.\n\n   - The prime factor \\(2\\) appears in both factorizations with the highest power \\(2^1\\).\n   - The prime factor \\(3\\) appears in both factorizations with the highest power \\(3^2\\).\n   - The prime factor \\(7\\) appears in the factorization of \\(42\\) but not in \\(18\\).\n\n   Therefore, the LCM of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{LCM} = 2^1 \\times 3^2 \\times 7^1 = 2 \\times 9 \\times 7 = 126\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the product of the GCD and the LCM:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Product} = \\text{GCD} \\times \\text{LCM} = 6 \\times 126\n   \\]\n\n5. **Perform the multiplication:**\n\n   \\[\n   6 \\times 126 = 756\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the product of the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of \\(18\\) and \\(42\\) is \\(\\boxed{756}\\).",
